  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/labeo_coubie_en/",
      "html": "<ul><li>Common name: African carp</li><li>Origin: Nigeria</li><li>Maximum length 75 cm, weight up to 5 kg; most specimens reach only 25-30 cm</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/labeo_coubie_en/",
      "html": "<p>This species can attain a maximum length of 75 cm and a weight of up to 5 kg. Of course such giants appear only every now and then. Most specimens become only 25-30 cm long. Nevertheless the fish is suited for large aquaria only.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/labeo_coubie_en/",
      "html": "<p>The great variety of species of Labeo from Africa is hardly known in the hobby. Even for scientists the determination is difficult. Only in 1990 a new species was detected in the Niger basin! This brings the number of Labeo species known from the Niger to four: Labeo parvus, L. roseopunctatus, L. senegalensis, and L. coubie. The fish were imported from Nigeria.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/labeo_coubie_en/",
      "html": "<p>Against conspecifics the fish are quarrelsome, as is known from the whole relationship of the Labeo and Epalzeorhynchus group. However, they do not hurt each other. One should keep them either solitary or in larger groups in the aquarium. It is very unlikely that two or three specimens together will do well in the long term.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/labeo_coubie_en/",
      "html": "<p>The smallest fish show a faded caudal spot, which is no longer present in larger conspecifics. The bigger the fish, the larger the dorsal fin; in the largest specimens it is a sail-shaped fin. At first glance the fish seem grey, but they look nice because of a purple-red spot on each scale. The membranes of the fins also have a purplish shine.</p><p>Lexicon: Labeo refers to the enlarged lips of the members of the genus. coubie: after the vernacular name of the species on the fish market in Cairo (Egypt), from where the type specimens originate.</p>"
    }
  ],
